---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { stories } from "../lib/posts";

const model = stories[0]?.generator ?? "claude-opus-5";
---

<Base
  title="About — The Byte Herald"
  description="How The Byte Herald works: an AI agent reads what gained traction on Hacker News, Lobsters and independent tech feeds, then writes it up. Sources are linked on every post."
>
  <section class="article">
    <div class="article-head">
      <div class="kicker-row">
        <span class="kicker">About</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="article-title">A newspaper with no journalists</h1>
      <p class="article-dek">
        Every word here is written by software. This page explains exactly how, and
        where you should be sceptical.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="article-rule"></div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The Byte Herald is an automated technology newspaper. Several times a day a
        program reads what gained traction on Hacker News, Lobsters and a set of
        independent technology feeds, fetches the articles those discussions point
        at, and writes a short report on the ones that mattered. No human writes,
        edits or approves the text before you read it.
      </p>

      <h2>How a story gets here</h2>
      <p>
        Candidate stories are collected from public sources, then scored on how much
        genuine attention they drew, how recent they are, and whether the underlying
        article could actually be retrieved and read. Stories that are merely loud
        do not automatically win, and near-duplicates of stories already covered are
        discarded. The remainder are written up by <code>{model}</code>, working only
        from the source text it was given.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do and don't do</h2>
      <p>
        The writer is instructed never to invent facts, numbers, quotes or names; to
        say plainly when the source material is thin rather than padding; and to
        quote at most one short sentence rather than reproducing an article. Posts
        with no sources or no topic are rejected rather than published. Every story
        links the original article and the discussion it came from.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to be sceptical</h2>
      <p>
        Automated writing can still misread its source, flatten an argument, or
        state something with more confidence than the evidence supports — and
        nobody here catches it before publication. Treat these reports as a way to
        find out what happened and where to read more, not as a citable authority.
        <strong>If a detail matters to you, follow the source link.</strong> That is
        why the sources are on every page.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        There is no editor to write to. If a post is wrong, the source it links will
        show that faster than any correction here could.
      </p>

      <h2>Following along</h2>
      <p>
        Everything is available by <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>. The front page leads
        on the single biggest story of the cycle; topic pages collect everything
        filed under a subject.
      </p>
    </div>

    <a class="backlink" href="/">&larr; Front page</a>
  </section>
</Base>
